cards.body:
The 2016 cyber fraud on Bangladesh Bank's account at the New York Fed involved unauthorised payment instructions worth about USD 81 million. Section 11.8 of today's ICT Security Guideline gives SWIFT its own rules. Banks must follow SWIFT's Customer Security Controls Framework and, among other things:

- **restrict internet access** on SWIFT servers and client PCs, and **restrict USB** use;
- allow **no super-user profiles**; admin and monitor profiles have no message processing rights; use **four- or six-eye** checks and maker-checker;
- make **multi-factor sign-on** mandatory;
- limit traffic to and from SWIFT servers **outside working hours**, such as holidays and after end of day;
- never store passwords in files on servers;
- make sure **every employee knows the incident response plan**;
- submit the **annual security attestation**, backed by an independent assessment each year by a SWIFT-listed provider.
